  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiet weather continues into the first half of\nthe week.\n\nThe tranquil Labor Day weather will continue into Tuesday under the \ninfluence of building high pressure at the surface and in the mid \nlevels. As the sprawling 500 mb ridge shifts east from the Ohio \nValley height rises will contribute to continued dry weather and a \nwarming airmass. 850 mb temps increase a few degrees to ~11C under \ndry NW flow. This will result in high temperatures just a few \ndegrees north of average, in the upper 70s (higher elevations and \nalong the coasts) to the low 80s). Dewpoints will be higher than \nMonday, but still comfortable in the mid to upper 50s. Under high \npressure winds will be light with sea breezes along the\ncoastline. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Warmer, breezy and more humid weather briefly \nreturns Wednesday into Thursday, with a cold front bringing a \nrisk for light rain showers Thursday, clearing offshore by \nThursday early evening.\n\nWe begin to turn warmer and also a bit more humid than recently by \nWednesday, ahead of a cold front which will very slowly progress \nsouthward into Southern New England by early Thursday morning. SW \nwinds will also pick up by Wednesday afternoon but especially \nWednesday night into early Thursday, particularly for the south \ncoast, Cape and Islands. 950 mb SW jet upwards of 50 kt is depicted \non the GFS along the south coast Wednesday night into Thursday. \nGusts Wednesday night along the south coast, Cape and Islands could \nreach up to 40 mph if we can mix, but thinking gusts clock in a \nlittle less since profiles are warming aloft. On the waters \npotential exists for gale headlines along the southern waters, but \ngusts over land don't seem worthy of headlines. \n\nEven though PWATs rise to around 1.5-1.8\", there isn't an especially \nstrong signal for significant rain as the front pushes southward on \nThursday. Most of the ensembles and AI-based guidance seem \npessimistic on rain chances, perhaps due to westerly flow at sfc and \naloft, so left a 25-30% PoP mention for Thursday with the front \nmoving southward/offshore by Thursday afternoon.  \n\nHighs Wednesday and Thursday should be similar both days in the \nupper 70s to mid 80s, but lows Wednesday night should be somewhat \nsticky with lows in the mid to upper 60s with dewpoints in the mid \nto upper 60s. More substantial cooling and drying takes place \nThursday night post-frontal, with lows in the mid 50s to lower 60s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Full sun, seasonable temperatures and onshore \nbreezes Friday into the weekend.\n\nA sprawling 1024 mb high pressure cell ridges into New England on \nFriday with moderately strong cool and dry advection and onshore NE \nwinds taking hold through Saturday night. Expecting full sun, low \nhumidity levels and dry weather Friday through Saturday night. \nAirmass starts to modify/warm up some into Sunday; increasing cloud \ncover by late Sunday/Sunday night ahead of a weak cool frontal \nboundary, but at least the daytime hours should end up largely dry. \n\nHighs Fri and Sat into the mid to upper 70s with low humidity \nlevels, except low 70s near the eastern coastlines. By Sunday highs \nshould top out in the mid 70s to low 80s despite increasing \ncloud cover.",
